% === provenance: Martin2003Agile ===
% CLAIM: The Single Responsibility Principle -- a class should have one,
%   and only one, reason to change -- is Robert C. Martin's.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '"single responsibility principle"' -n
%   variables-srp -p openalex -p dblp -p wos -p ieee -p scopus (query S1);
%   the book itself is not indexed by those providers as the SRP source,
%   so it was retrieved directly: the chapter text from the author's own
%   Object Mentor chapter PDF via the Internet Archive
%   (https://web.archive.org/web/20150202200348/http://www.objectmentor.com/resources/articles/srp.pdf),
%   and the published book's chapter numbering, publisher, year and ISBN
%   from the publisher's own catalogue page (WebFetch
%   https://www.informit.com/store/agile-software-development-principles-patterns-and-9780135974445).
% PICKED: this is the primary statement of SRP and the source the
%   secondary literature attributes it to (see Ardalani2024Supporting,
%   which cites "(Martin, 2003)" for exactly this formulation).  The
%   obvious rival primary candidate, Martin's 2000 paper "Design
%   Principles and Design Patterns" (Martin2000Design), was checked and
%   does NOT contain SRP -- see its provenance block.  Note the source is
%   a trade book, not a peer-reviewed publication.
% QUOTE (ch. 8; Object Mentor chapter PDF p. 149): "SRP: The Single
%   Responsibility Principle / THERE SHOULD NEVER BE MORE THAN ONE
%   REASON FOR A CLASS TO CHANGE."
%   Also (p. 151), defining the term the claim turns on: "In the context
%   of the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) we define a
%   responsibility to be \mkbibquote{a reason for change.}  If you can
%   think of more than one motive for changing a class, then that class
%   has more than one responsibility."  Ancestry, stated by Martin
%   himself (p. 149): "This principle was described in the work of Tom
%   DeMarco and Meilir Page-Jones.  They called it cohesion." (footnotes
%   to [DeMarco79] p. 310 and [PageJones88] ch. 6 p. 82).  Martin's own
%   caveat (p. 153): "The SRP is one of the simplest of the principle,
%   and one of the hardest to get right."
% VERIFIED: full chapter text read (Object Mentor chapter PDF, 6 pp.,
%   archived copy).  PARTIAL LOCATOR CAVEAT: the archived chapter PDF
%   carries draft numbering (chapter 9, pp. 149-154, ISP in ch. 13,
%   package cohesion in ch. 21); the published book numbers these
%   chapters 8, 12 and 20 respectively, per the publisher's table of
%   contents.  The published book's page numbers were NOT verified, so
%   cite the chapter ("ch. 8"), never a page of the printed book.
% COUNTER: scholar search '("single responsibility principle" OR "SOLID
%   principles") AND (criticism OR critique OR vague OR ambiguous OR
%   "ill-defined" OR subjective OR "not well defined")' -n variables-srp
%   on all five live providers (query C1; openalex 40, wos 5, ieee 0,
%   scopus 5, dblp 0 hits) surfaced no work disputing that SRP is
%   Martin's.  No rival claimant to the principle was found in any
%   query.  What the counter-searches did surface is dispute about the
%   principle's CONTENT, not its authorship: Loth2026Why argues the
%   formulation is false, and Martin2014SRP is Martin himself conceding
%   that "reason to change" begs the question.  Attribution stands;
%   see those entries for the qualification.
% FOUND-VIA (here): backed in föreläsningen Funktioner (modules/variables/
%   slides-functions, bilaga B); entry and provenance copied verbatim.
%   This deck applies the principle to a module (typed_input has one
%   responsibility, input) rather than a class; Martin's own scope is
%   "class", and the deck says "liksom en funktion" without claiming
%   Martin wrote about modules.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@book{Martin2003Agile,
  author    = {Martin, Robert C.},
  title     = {Agile Software Development, Principles, Patterns, and Practices},
  publisher = {Prentice Hall},
  address   = {Upper Saddle River, NJ},
  year      = {2003},
  isbn      = {0-13-597444-5},
  note      = {Chapter 8, \mkbibquote{SRP: The Single-Responsibility Principle}}
}

