% ===========================================================================
% Facts about Python: the language's own documentation as primary source.

% === provenance: PyDocModules ===
% CLAIM: (1) A module is a file containing Python definitions and
%   statements, named after the file without .py; (2) each module has its
%   own namespace, so a module's names cannot clash with the importing
%   program's; (3) importing executes the module's statements, once per
%   interpreter session, and running it as a script executes them too,
%   with __name__ set to "__main__"; (4) "from m import *" imports all
%   names not starting with an underscore and is discouraged because it
%   hides names already defined; (5) "import m as n" binds the module to
%   the name n; (6) the script's own directory is searched before the
%   standard library, so a same-named file shadows a library module;
%   (7) compiled modules are cached in __pycache__; (8) a package
%   structures modules with dotted names.
% FOUND-VIA: known document -- the official Python tutorial, chapter 6.
%   WebFetch https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/modules.html (2026-09-03,
%   three fetches with different extraction prompts; the page reports
%   itself as the Python 3.14.7 documentation).
% PICKED: the language's own documentation is the primary source for
%   facts about the language.  The tutorial is preferred over the
%   Language Reference's import chapter because the tutorial states each
%   fact in one sentence at the level the deck teaches, and the deck
%   cites it by section number so the reader can find the passage.
% QUOTE (6): "A module is a file containing Python definitions and
%   statements. The file name is the module name with the suffix .py
%   appended. Within a module, the module's name (as a string) is
%   available as the value of the global variable __name__."
% QUOTE (6.1): "A module can contain executable statements as well as
%   function definitions. These statements are intended to initialize
%   the module. They are executed only the first time the module name is
%   encountered in an import statement. (They are also run if the file
%   is executed as a script.)"
% QUOTE (6.1): "Each module has its own private namespace, which is used
%   as the global namespace by all functions defined in the module.
%   Thus, the author of a module can use global variables in the module
%   without worrying about accidental clashes with a user's global
%   variables."
% QUOTE (6): "If the module name is followed by as, then the name
%   following as is bound directly to the imported module."
% QUOTE (6): "This imports all names except those beginning with an
%   underscore (_). In most cases Python programmers do not use this
%   facility since it introduces an unknown set of names into the
%   interpreter, possibly hiding some things you have already defined."
% QUOTE (6.1.1): "the code in the module will be executed, just as if
%   you imported it, but with the __name__ set to \"__main__\"."
% QUOTE (6.1.2): "When a module named spam is imported, the interpreter
%   first searches for a built-in module with that name." ... "The
%   directory containing the script being run is placed at the beginning
